Q: Why does a non-match on an empty string with PHP&#39;s sscanf() return -1 instead of 0? A: <p>All the way at the bottom of <code>PHPAPI in php_scanf_internal()</code> @ <a href="https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/1603252e41a0085024b6f715ba698a7e47875924/ext/standard/scanf.c#L1168" rel="nofollow noreferrer">php-src/ext/standard
/scanf.c</a> there is a condition that mirrors the inherited behavior of C's <code>scanf()</code>. Effectively, if the input string is exhausted before matching could even begin, <code>SCAN_ERROR_EOF</code> is returned - which is a constant that equals <code>-1</code>. So the bug in the userland coding attempt is an &quot;underflow&quot; problem.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>...
done:
    result = SCAN_SUCCESS;

    if (underflow &amp;&amp; (0==nconversions)) {
        scan_set_error_return( numVars, return_value );
        result = SCAN_ERROR_EOF;
    } else if (numVars) {
        zval_ptr_dtor(return_value );
        ZVAL_LONG(return_value, nconversions);
    } else if (nconversions &lt; totalVars) {
        /* TODO: not all elements converted. we need to prune the list - cc */
    }
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p>To explain the behavior simply: <a href="https://3v4l.org/hbI7n#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo with more tests</a></p>
<div class="s-table-container"><table class="s-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>code</th>
<th>return</th>
<th><code>$num</code></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>echo sscanf('', '%d', $num);</code></td>
<td><code>-1</code></td>
<td><code>null</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>echo sscanf('foo', '%d', $num);</code></td>
<td><code>0</code></td>
<td><code>null</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>echo sscanf('411', '%d', $num);</code></td>
<td><code>1</code></td>
<td><code>411</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>In the provided example, simply check if the return value is greater than 0 instead of making a truthy check and the ternary expression will evaluate as intended.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$details = sscanf($userInput ?? '', '%d', $id)) &gt; 0 ? $this-&gt;fetchDetails($id) : null;
</code></pre>
